>>
Rule 1 (mode must be a boolean literal)
{v_1 : bool} |= (v_1 = true) ∨ (v_1 = false)
>>
Rule 2 (mode takes exactly one of two boolean states)
{v_1 : bool} |= ((v_1 = true) ∧ (v_1 ≠ false)) ∨ ((v_1 = false) ∧ (v_1 ≠ true))
>>
Rule 3 (the call is well-defined for either mode value)
{v_1 : bool} |= if v_1 then true
>>
Rule 4 (mode deterministically selects enable or disable)
{v_1 : bool} |= if v_1 then v_1 = true else v_1 = false
>>
Rule 5 (enabling implies not disabling)
{v_1 : bool} |= if v_1 then v_1 ≠ false
>>
Rule 6 (disabling implies not enabling)
{v_1 : bool} |= if v_1 then true else v_1 ≠ true
>>
Rule 7 (mode equals the evaluated boolean literal)
{v_1 : bool} |= if v_1 then ((if v_1 then true else false) = true) else ((if v_1 then true else false) = false)
>>
Rule 8 (for any dummy index, mode resolves to a literal state)
{v_1 : bool} |= ∀i ∈ [0, 0] : if v_1 then v_1 = true else v_1 = false
